use hope really more as a framework for action. Dr. Mattis made the comment at the very beginning that I love, hope is a firm. It is about taking action. And so it's the idea of how do we begin the process of client-centered or person-centered goal setting. Oftentimes the system infuses the goals that clients need to navigate through. But this is a process where they are having a a voice within the context of goals. So I'm talking about parents who have had their children removed and their system involved. So at what point do they get to be involved in setting goals for themselves and their families and then how our program services pathways to helping them achieve those kinds of goals?

